Set is a pre-defined interface in java.util package introduced from JDK 1.2.
> It stores objects in the form of Key-Value pairs in a Hashtable. 
> It is not Index based.
> Duplicate values are not allowed but null values are allowed. HashSet and LinkedHashSet allows a single null value(as adding another null value will create a duplicate)
while TreeSet don't allow any null values because it needs to compare them for sorting.

It has following implementation classes

i) HashSet -> Pre-defined class in java.util introduced from JDK 1.2, doesn't maintain the insertion order. Initial capacity - 16 and Load factor - 0.75 or 75%.
Underlined data structure is Hashtable, stores Objects(Values) along with their HashCodes(Keys).

ii) LinkedHashSet -> Pre-defined class in java.util introduced from JDK 1.4. It maintains the insertion order. No initial capacity and incremental capacity. 
Underlined data structure is Hashtable and LinkedList.

iii) TreeSet -> Pre-defined class in java.util introduced from JDK 1.2. It maintain the sorted order(increasing order). Underlined data structure is Binary tree.
It calls compareTo() method to compare and sort the values.

compareTo() method :-
returns +1 if new object > previous object -> Object moves to the right hand side of the previous object.
returns -1 if new object < previous object -> Object moves to the left hand side of the previous object.
returns 0 if new object = previous object  -> Object is not added in the binary tree. 

Sorting order = left node -> parent node -> right node
